NAME
       siem - search the practice SIEM's alerts and logs (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       siem alerts
       siem search [TERM...] [--fields A,B,C] [--sort FIELD] [--count-by FIELD]
                   [--limit N] [--reverse] [--since TIME] [--until TIME]
       siem show ID
       siem fields

DESCRIPTION
       A SIEM ("security information and event management") is the place a
       security team collects every computer's logs, so they can search them
       all at once and get alerts when something looks wrong. siem is Cyber
       Range's pretend one. Every event is a list of fields such as
       time=..., host=... (the computer that wrote it down), source=...,
       user=..., image=... (the program), and parent=... (the program that
       started it).

       An alert is only a starting point: a rule noticed a pattern. Many
       alerts turn out to be harmless (a "false alarm" or "false positive");
       the events around an alert tell you whether it is real.

       Sources you will meet:
         alert     an alert one of the SIEM's rules raised (rule, severity,
                   note); its host is the computer the alert is about
         winsec    Windows security log events: event=4624 a sign-in worked,
                   4625 a sign-in failed, 4634 signed out, 4672 an
                   administrator signed in, 4688 a program started, 4720 an
                   account was made, 4723 a person changed their password,
                   4728 / 4732 someone was added to a group, 4740 an account
                   was locked, 5145 a shared file was opened, 7045 a service
                   was installed, 1102 the log was cleared. logon_type says
                   how: 2 at the keyboard, 3 over the network (a shared
                   folder), 7 unlocking the screen, 10 remote desktop.
         proclog   program starts recorded by a process-logging tool
                   (event=1: image, parent, cmdline, user)
         dns       name lookups (query = the name asked for, answer = the
                   address it got)
         firewall  connections the firewall allowed or blocked (action,
                   direction, src, dst, dport = the port, bytes)
         auth      Linux sign-ins (sshd, sudo)
         web       web server requests (client, method, path, status,
                   agent = what the visitor's program says it is)

COMMANDS
       alerts
              List the alerts: id, time, severity, rule, host, note.

       search TERM...
              Show the events that match every TERM (all of them must hold):
                field=value    the field is exactly value (any case); a * in
                               value matches anything (image=*powershell*)
                field!=value   the field is not value (or the event has no
                               such field)
                field~text     the field contains text
                word           any field contains word
              The results are in time order, oldest first, and the last line
              says how many events matched.

       show ID
              Every field of one event (IDs are the #numbers in the results).

       fields
              The fields the events have, how many have each, and the sources.

OPTIONS
       --fields A,B,C   show only these fields, as a table
       --sort FIELD     sort by a field (the default is time)
       --count-by FIELD count the matching events for each value of FIELD
       --limit N        show only the first N
       --reverse        newest (or largest) first
       --since TIME     only events at or after TIME (HH:MM, HH:MM:SS, or a
                        whole time such as 2026-10-20T09:15:00)
       --until TIME     only events at or before TIME

EXAMPLES
           siem alerts
           siem search host=WS-07 source=proclog
           siem search source=winsec event=4625 --count-by user
           siem search parent=*winword* --fields time,host,user,image,cmdline
           siem search host=WS-07 --since 09:10 --until 09:30
           siem search source=dns query~invoice --limit 5
           siem show 42

       Base64 text (for example after -EncodedCommand) can be decoded on your
       own box with: echo 'TEXT' | base64 -d

       Everything siem shows is pretend data made by Cyber Range; it never
       reads a real computer's logs.

SEE ALSO
       journalctl(1), grep(1), base64(1)
